    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `extract_invoice` in `app/invoices.py`. The existing tests pass (2 of 2), but neither one calls `extract_invoice`, and I haven't run it against the live API. So the new code hasn't actually been exercised yet.\n\n**What it does:**\n- It sends one request through `client.beta.messages.create` with `MODEL`, `max_tokens=1024`, `betas=[FILES_BETA]` and the invoice as a document block, the same way `summarize_invoice` does.\n- It uses structured outputs: `output_config={\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": INVOICE_SCHEMA}}`. The API constrains the reply text to the schema, so the prompt is just a short instruction and doesn't describe the JSON. The installed SDK's beta `create` accepts `output_config`, and the schema already sets `additionalProperties: false` and lists every field as required, which structured outputs needs.\n- It joins the text blocks of the reply and returns them decoded with `json.loads`. Only text blocks are used because Opus 5.5 always thinks, so the reply may also contain thinking blocks.\n\n**One addition you didn't ask for:** if `stop_reason` is anything other than `end_turn`, the function raises a `RuntimeError` instead of trying to decode the text. The schema only holds for a complete answer, so a refusal or a reply cut off at 1024 tokens could return text that isn't valid JSON or doesn't match the schema. This way the ledger import gets a clear error instead of a broken row.\n\nA test that mocks the client and checks the request (the `output_config`, the document block and the beta flag) would cover this path; I can add one if you want."
  },
